            <ArticleTitle>Sensory changes after tongue reduction for macroglossia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e1-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.oooo.2012.02.037</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2212-4403(12)00298-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report sensory changes after tongue reduction by the Harada-Enomoto method for macroglossia in a 20-year-old woman with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. Sensory tests were performed before surgery and 1 week and 2 months after surgery. We assessed the static tactile threshold, vibration sense, static 2-point discrimination, pain threshold, and taste. No sensory loss of any category tested was observed after tongue reduction.</AbstractText>
